# dispatch-secrets.env -- credentials (mode 0600, never committed)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ============================================================================
# NEVER QUOTE ANY VALUE IN THIS FILE. NO EXCEPTIONS, EVER, FOR ANY REASON.
# (established 2026-09-05, after quoting broke this twice for real: NWWS-OI
#  auth failed silently for ~1 hour, and AMTRAK_CORE_ROUTES route-matching
#  broke, both from a single stray pair of quote characters.)
#
# Every real consumer of this file treats the entire remainder of a line
# after "=" as the LITERAL value, quotes included:
#   - Every Podman Quadlet .container unit's EnvironmentFile= directive is
#     translated by Quadlet into `podman run --env-file <this file>` --
#     it is PODMAN's env-file parser that actually runs, never systemd's
#     (confirmed live 2026-09-05: `podman exec ... env` showed the raw
#     quote characters INSIDE the running container's own environment).
#     Podman's --env-file format does zero quote-stripping and zero shell
#     word-splitting -- a value with a comma or a space is preserved
#     perfectly fine unquoted; a value WITH quotes gets the quote
#     characters baked in as part of the value, corrupting it.
#   - Every shell script in scripts/ that reads this file uses the
#     `read_env_var()` grep+cut pattern (see container-mem-watch.sh,
#     scheduled-ingest-restart.sh) specifically so no quoting is ever
#     needed -- nothing in this repo bash-`source`s this file anymore.
#     If you're tempted to quote a value "because it has a space," that
#     need doesn't exist here -- fix the script that reads it (make it
#     use read_env_var(), never `source`) instead of quoting the file.
#
# If you ever hit an actual bash `source` of this file (a one-off manual
# script, an interactive shell) and it mis-splits an unquoted value with a
# space: that is the script's bug, not this file's -- fix the script (use
# read_env_var()-style grep+cut, or a proper env-file parser), do not
# quote the value here. Quoting here is silently, catastrophically wrong
# for every real consumer that matters. This is enforced by
# scripts/check-env-quoting.sh (wired into the pre-commit hook and the
# integrity sweep) -- a quoted value in a tracked env file fails CI/commit
# before it can reach production again.
# ============================================================================
#
# DO NOT edit this file manually on the Pi.
# Use populate-secrets.sh to write values from ~/.secrets/{name}.{token,key}
#
# Secrets file naming convention:
#   ~/.secrets/ntfy.token         -> NTFY_TOKEN
#   ~/.secrets/acarsdrama.token   -> ACARSDRAMA_JUMPSEAT_TOKEN
#   ~/.secrets/airframes.token    -> AIRFRAMES_TOKEN
#   ~/.secrets/kpler.key         -> KPLER_MARITIME_API_TOKEN (Maritime 2.0 GraphQL)
#   ~/.secrets/marinetraffic.key  -> AIS_MARINETRAFFIC_KEY (embed widget only -- separate product)
#   ~/.secrets/aishub.key         -> AIS_AISHUB_ID
#   ~/.secrets/vesselfi.key       -> AIS_VESSELFI_KEY
#
# NOTE: No LLM API key is required for the default local setup.
#       This platform runs inference via Ollama on-device. See dispatch.env for
#       OLLAMA_BASE_URL / OLLAMA_CHAT_MODEL / OLLAMA_OSINT_MODEL.
#
#       Cloud LLM API keys (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google Gemini, etc.) are ONLY
#       needed if you choose to replace Ollama with a cloud provider — that
#       is an opt-in deviation from the intended architecture.
#
# For signup instructions and email templates for every source below, see:
#   docs/DATA_SOURCES.md
# For regional equivalents (European/Asia-Pacific feeds), see:
#   docs/REGIONALIZATION.md
#
# Run: bash populate-secrets.sh
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# =============================================================================
# CORE (required for basic operation)
# =============================================================================

# ntfy auth token
# Self-hosted ntfy — generate with: ntfy user add --role=admin <user>
NTFY_TOKEN=CHANGE_ME

# PostgreSQL password for the dispatch write path (2026-09-06, see
# docs/POSTGRES_MIGRATION.md). MUST equal POSTGRES_PASSWORD in
# /etc/corporatetraveldc/pgsql-secrets.env (the server's own dedicated
# secrets file -- kept separate so the postgres container never sees the
# SWIM/NWWS/API credentials in THIS file). Host/port/db/user are non-secret
# and live in dispatch.env (DISPATCH_PG_*). Generate with:
#   openssl rand -hex 24
DISPATCH_PG_PASSWORD=CHANGE_ME



# =============================================================================
# RECEIVER LOCATION (highly encouraged — see below)
# =============================================================================

# Not a credential, but it lives here rather than dispatch.env because a
# precise home address is meaningfully more sensitive than ordinary config
# and this file is 0600/never committed, same reasoning as everything else
# on this page. If you leave these unset, the platform silently falls back
# to a generic Washington-DC-area placeholder (see DEFAULT_LAT/DEFAULT_LON
# in src/runner/main.py and _ADSB_DEFAULT_LAT/_ADSB_DEFAULT_LON in
# src/web/main.py) — it will run, but every distance-from-you calculation,
# the compass-quadrant summary, the tactical map's range rings, and (most
# visibly) the ADS-B embed's native "H"/home key and per-aircraft distance
# columns will all be silently wrong for YOUR location. If you're running
# a real ADS-B receiver (UltraFeeder/readsb), set these to its actual
# antenna coordinates — MLAT accuracy also depends on this being correct,
# not just the display. Never hardcode a real coordinate directly into a
# tracked source file instead of here — see CLAUDE.md's 2026-08-24
# GPS-coordinate-confusion writeup for why that specific mistake is worth
# avoiding (four independent tracked-file literals disagreed with each
# other and with the real location before this was centralized).
#
# Consumed by:
#   - the UltraFeeder Quadlet (READSB_LAT/READSB_LON/TAR1090_DEFAULTCENTERLAT/
#     TAR1090_DEFAULTCENTERLON — set those too, or point them at the same
#     values, if you're running your own receiver rather than pulling
#     someone else's aggregator feed)
#   - runner's /api/v1/frontend-config endpoint, which is the ONLY place
#     the React frontend should ever read this from (never a hardcoded
#     literal in a .jsx/.js file)
ULTRAFEEDER_LAT=
ULTRAFEEDER_LON=



